## 1. Publish the Protean jar to mavenLocal (SNAPSHOT / not-yet-on-Central only)

From the **protean repo**, once (and again whenever the library changes):

```bash
cd <protean repo>
./gradlew publishToMavenLocal
```

If the coordinate is a released version already on Maven Central, skip this step.

## 2. Start the database (data-access samples only)

```bash
docker compose up -d
```

Override the target with `DB_HOST` / `DB_PORT` / `DB_USER` / `DB_PASSWORD` (see `application.yml`).

## 3. Run the server

```bash
./gradlew run
```

Override the HTTP port with `SERVER_PORT` (it drives both `server.port` and the advertised MCP address, so the two
cannot drift). `./setup.sh` uses the same variable.

## 4. Deploy a module over MCP (when MCP is enabled)

> **MCP is an RCE surface** — it compiles and hot-loads submitted sources. This sample enables it with
> permissive authorization for a **local demo only**. For anything real, add authentication (Bearer/OAuth via
> Spring Security) and a `ModuleActionAuthorizer` policy — see [§Security](#security).

List the tools:

```bash
curl -s localhost:8080/platform/mcp -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/list"}'
```

Deploy a module (`deploy_module` carries sources in `files[]`; FQCN = package + filename). Over the raw JSON-RPC
endpoint the tool name uses a **dot** (`protean.deploy_module`), not the underscore of the MCP client tool.

```bash
curl -s localhost:8080/platform/mcp -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{
  "jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/call","params":{
    "name":"protean.deploy_module",
    "arguments":{
      "id":"hello","version":"1","controller":"gen.HelloController",
      "files":[
        {"kind":"source","filename":"HelloController.java","content":"package gen;\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;\n@RestController\npublic class HelloController {\n  public static String up(String s){ return s.toUpperCase(); }\n  @GetMapping(\"/hello\")\n  public String hello(@RequestParam(defaultValue=\"world\") String name){ return \"hello \"+up(name); }\n}\n"},
        {"kind":"test","filename":"HelloTest.java","content":"package gen;\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;\nimport static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;\nclass HelloTest {\n  @Test void up(){ assertEquals(\"HI\", HelloController.up(\"hi\")); }\n}\n"}
      ]
    }
  }}'
```

**Streaming and WebSocket.** A module may return an `SseEmitter` — nothing here restricts it to request/response,
and `protean.module.request-timeout-ms` does not cut an async stream short. Prefer `isolation.mode=in-process` for
it: under worker/container the reverse proxy relays the body without flushing, so small events can sit in the
buffer, and never declare a verify probe on a streaming path (the probe has no read timeout and would hang the
deploy). **WebSocket/STOMP cannot be a module route** — protean registers module endpoints as request mappings, and
WebSocket handlers have no runtime-registration path; put a WS endpoint in this host app instead.

### Module authoring rules

<!-- [interface_spec_validator ONLY] Drop this block when that capability was not selected — with no
     amoeba.define_interface there is no generated skeleton and nothing below applies. -->
**Declare the contract first, then write only what the server cannot derive.** Modules here are generated:
`amoeba.define_interface` takes an `InterfaceSpec` and returns a full skeleton.

| Server-owned — rewritten from the spec on every deploy | Author-owned |
|---|---|
| `<Base>Controller.java` | `<Base>Service.java` (or `<Base>ServiceImpl.java`) |
| `<Base>Service.java` (the interface, when `service-interface: true`) | `mapper/<module>/<Base>Mapper.xml` — **the SQL** |
| every request/response DTO + one `<Op>Row` per read operation | `<Base>ContractTest.java` |
| **`<Base>Mapper.java`** · `<Base>MyBatisConfig.java` | |

**Ask the server, do not trust this table.** Read `files[].serverOwned` from the `define_interface` response — it is
the only authority, and it changes with `application.yml`. Do **not** trust a generated file's header either: a
header once said "AUTHORED. Add methods freely" on a file that was already server-owned, and everything added to it
was discarded. `SkeletonGeneratorTest#ownershipSplitIsAsDeclared` pins the split in code.

**Read `replacedFiles` and `seededFiles` in the deploy response** — the only way to learn your work was thrown away.
One of *your* files in `replacedFiles` means you got its **filename** wrong. `<Base>Service`/`ServiceImpl` in
`seededFiles` means the throwing stub shipped and gate 3 will roll the deploy back.

> **★ A generated service stub throws `UnsupportedOperationException`, and deploying it untouched is SUPPOSED to
> fail.** Gate 3 probes the declared routes right after install, the stub throws, and protean undeploys and rolls
> the module back — naming the operation still stubbed. That is the policy working.

**The Mapper interface is server-owned, so you cannot add methods to it.** Two consequences that changed how real
modules were written:

- **Duplicate detection is a DB unique constraint**, not a pre-read: catch `DuplicateKeyException` → 409. That also
  removes the race between checking and inserting. The unique key in your DDL is then **the only thing enforcing
  it** — drop it and duplicates enter silently.
- **Read-after-write goes through the existing search method plus an exact-match filter.** A `LIKE %CODE001%` also
  matches `CODE0010`; without the filter you return the wrong row. Pin that boundary in the contract test.
- A **list operation** gets a `count<Op>` method generated alongside it, because one method cannot return both the
  rows and the total and you cannot add the second one yourself.

**Gate 2 is a bytecode check — it cannot be worked around.** `@Schema` is required on `@Mapper` abstract methods
and on `*Request`/`*Response`/`*Row` record components; `ResponseEntity`, `Map` and `Object` are rejected as return
types; and an inner type must not END with those three suffixes (nested names like `Foo$Row` are checked too).

**After deploying, check `desiredState: ACTIVE` AND `boundGeneration != null`.** With a null generation the state
reads ACTIVE while no route is bound, so every endpoint 404s — and that 404 is the server's, not your module's,
which makes it read like a client wiring problem.

**Routes mount at the root, REST-style.** `GET /cars`, not `GET /api/car/cars`. A UI's `/api` prefix is the proxy's
business, not the module's.

**A list response is `{items, total, page, size}`**, where `total` is the count *before* paging. Share the column
list and the WHERE with `<sql>` so the page query and the count query cannot drift apart when a filter is added.
Compute the offset with `<bind name="offset" value="page * size"/>` — MySQL's `LIMIT` takes no expression, and
`${}` is an injection surface. Map records with an explicit `<constructor>`: automatic constructor mapping depends
on whether `-parameters` was passed and fails quietly when it was not.

**Declared success statuses are realised.** A spec that says `201` or `204` gets `@ResponseStatus` on the generated
handler, so REST conventions work and the gate-3 probe sees the same status the spec declared.

**Gate-3 probes are generated only for non-mutating operations.** `DELETE`, `PUT` and `PATCH` are never probed
automatically — a probe is a real request, and an auto-generated `DELETE` deleted a live row on every deploy before
this rule existed. Route registration is still verified separately, so nothing is lost. A `GET` probe **does** run,
and it substitutes the path parameter's `example`: **that value must exist at deploy time**, so give the table a
probe-only reserved row and name it in `example`. To probe a mutation deliberately, pass it yourself in
`verification.integration` — a caller-supplied probe replaces the auto-generated one for the same method and path.

<!-- [/interface_spec_validator ONLY] -->

**Extend the base types this app provides.** In the MVC shape (Controller → Service → Mapper) the Service and the
Mapper inherit from `src/main/java/{{PKG_PATH}}/support/`:

```java
public class OrdersService extends {{PKG}}.support.BaseService { ... }

@Mapper
public interface OrdersMapper extends {{PKG}}.support.BaseMapper { ... }
```

Both are empty. They exist so a cross-cutting concern can be added in one file later and reach every module,
instead of requiring an edit to each module that was already deployed. The Controller extends nothing — its seam
is `CommonInterceptor`, which already sees module routes.

**`@Transactional` goes on the Service. Not the Mapper, not the Controller.** A MyBatis Mapper is a proxy
generated by `MapperFactoryBean`, so the annotation there is not honoured — it looks like a boundary and is not
one. On a Controller the transaction would wrap request parsing and response serialisation, holding the connection
for the whole exchange.

> **`@Transactional` is silently ignored until your module enables it.** A module runs in a child
> `ApplicationContext`; `@EnableTransactionManagement` and Boot's transaction autoconfiguration ran in the *host*
> context, and Spring's BeanPostProcessors are per-context — so the host's transaction advisor never proxies a
> module bean. The method just runs, without a transaction and without an error.
>
> **Design so you do not need one: one use case = one write statement.** Needing several is itself a signal to
> revisit the design, because the annotation that looks like it protects you does nothing.
>
> The two escape hatches are **conditional on this project's configuration**, and under a pinned skeleton
> (`amoeba.skeleton.spec-overrides-allowed: false`) the first one does not exist:
>
> | Escape hatch | Available? |
> |---|---|
> | Your module adds `@EnableTransactionManagement` + a `PlatformTransactionManager` in its own `@Configuration` | **No** under a pinned skeleton — `components` is filled from the skeleton, so a `@Configuration` you submit is never registered |
> | Inject the host's `PlatformTransactionManager` and use a `TransactionTemplate` | Yes **while `needsSharedBeans` is true** (it is, whenever the skeleton is MyBatis) |
>
> Four workarounds were tried on a real module and all four failed: submitting `<X>MyBatisConfig` (server-owned →
> discarded), adding a differently-named `@Configuration` (not in `components` → not registered), returning `Map`
> from the Mapper (gate 2 rejects it), and `dataAccess: none` with hand-wired MyBatis (`needsSharedBeans` false →
> no host `DataSource`).
>
> Scope of the transaction once it works: in-process with this app's `DataSource` and transaction manager →
> participates in the host transaction. In-process with a `DataSource` the module creates → independent. Under
> `worker`/`container` isolation → a separate process, so always independent.

The bundled test runs as a **deploy gate**; it must pass for the module to reach `ACTIVE`. Then:

```bash
curl 'localhost:8080/hello?name=protean'      # → hello PROTEAN
curl localhost:8080/platform/modules          # deployment state
```

