## Security

The MCP control plane (`/platform/**`) is a **remote code execution surface**: it compiles and hot-loads submitted
sources. `SecurityConfig` puts an OAuth2 Resource Server in front of it, so every call needs a Bearer token, and
`ExampleAuthorizer` decides each `ModuleAction` on top of that.

### Choosing the issuer value

`spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri` is bound to `${OAUTH_ISSUER_URI}` **with no default** — the
app refuses to start without a usable value.

The rule: it must equal, character for character, the `iss` claim string in the tokens your Authorization Server
issues. **Not** the token endpoint, the JWKS URL, the login page, or the admin console. Don't guess — confirm it:

```bash
curl -s https://<candidate>/.well-known/openid-configuration | jq -r .issuer
```

The output must be identical to your candidate; if it differs, use **the output**. Cross-check against a real
token's payload (JWT's second segment, base64url):

```bash
echo "$TOKEN" | cut -d. -f2 | tr '_-' '/+' | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | jq -r .iss
```

Starting points per vendor (still confirm with the command above): Keycloak 17+ `https://<host>/realms/<realm>` ·
Auth0 `https://<tenant>.<region>.auth0.com/` (trailing slash included) · Okta
`https://<domain>/oauth2/<authServerId>` · Entra ID `https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenantId>/v2.0` ·
Spring Authorization Server = whatever issuer you configured (default: the app's base URL).

### Symptoms of a wrong value

The first two rows **start the app successfully** — you find out after deployment, via 401s. That is why the
confirmation step above comes first.

| Value | Symptom |
|---|---|
| `iss` mismatch (trailing slash, etc.) | Starts fine, **every request 401** — signature verifies, `iss` check fails |
| Unreachable host | Starts fine, first token throws `JwtDecoderInitializationException` → 401 |
| No scheme / empty | **Refuses to start** (`SecurityConfig` constructor) |
| Unset | **Refuses to start** (Spring cannot resolve the placeholder) |

A well-formed but wrong or unreachable issuer is deliberately not rejected at startup: Boot's `issuer-uri` support
returns a `SupplierJwtDecoder` that defers the discovery fetch to the first token, so the AS need not be reachable
when the app boots.

### Reachable from both sides

`protean.mcp.authorization.resource` and `protean.mcp.authorization.authorization-servers` are **advertised** at
`/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`, which is public. So they must be addresses reachable by the **MCP client**,
not just by this server — an internal-only hostname boots fine and validates tokens fine while leaving clients
unable to obtain one. Both are built from `SERVER_HOST`/`SERVER_PORT` for that reason.

> **Known limitation** — the advertised scheme is fixed to `http`. Behind TLS termination this advertises `http://`
> and a client that string-compares the resource identifier will reject it; a standard-port deployment also still
> gets an explicit `:443`/`:80` in the URL. Switch that line in `application.yml` to `https` when you get there.

